WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Secure RTOS design begins with clear boundaries.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:13.000
Define task responsibilities, driver responsibilities, peripheral interfaces, interrupt paths, shared resources, and privileged code.

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.220
When the architecture shows where data and authority move, review becomes practical.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.180
Keep memory and communication contracts explicit.

5
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:30.840
Know who owns each buffer, how long it is valid, what length and capacity apply, which task may mutate it, and how messages are validated.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:38.380
Predictable memory behavior and clear inter-task communication reduce both reliability and security risk.

7
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.180
Minimize high-authority and timing-sensitive work.

8
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:51.000
Keep interrupt handlers small, defer complex work, limit privileged access, control shared state, and size stacks for real workloads.

9
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:57.180
Real-time behavior is easier to trust when the most sensitive paths are small and well tested.

10
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.020
Finally, test operational behavior.

11
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:11.720
Exercise fault paths, timeout paths, queue full conditions, stack margins, update failures, watchdog behavior, diagnostic limits, and production configuration.

12
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:24.300
RTOS security is a set of habits: explicit boundaries, least privilege, memory discipline, safe communication, interrupt discipline, hardened configuration, and tested failure behavior.
